  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms are possible Tuesday \namongst hot and humid conditions.\n\nSome guidance shows additional showers and perhaps a\nthunderstorm moving through the area overnight or into Tuesday \nmorning, though this will be dependent on what develops \nupstream. It's unlikely any of this activity would be severe.\n\nProspects for thunderstorm continue Tuesday but confidence in\nthe details is lower. There is some signal in the models that\ninstability will be lower over much of the forecast area since\nthe atmosphere might be a bit overturned and there could be\ndebris cloudiness around. Severe weather probabilities are\nhighest across southwestern portions of the CWA, which may be in\ncloser proximity to the MCS track coming out of the Ohio Valley.\nSome models do still show thunderstorms farther north in the\narea as well, though. Damaging winds will again be the primary\nthreat.\n\nThe risk for isolated flash flooding could occur anywhere on\neither day if a merging or backbuilding storm were to occur \nover a susceptible area. Accumulatively, however, the risk of \nflooding is greatest near and west of the Appalachians where \nseveral rounds of heavy thunderstorms may occur through mid week.\n\nToday might be the hottest day of the week as heat index values\nare locally peaking over 100. While the humidity sticks around\nTuesday, temperatures may be slightly lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms remain possible at\ntimes through the end of the week, with a cooling trend \nexpected toward the weekend.\n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Friday as a \ncold front gradually drifts south. Ahead of that front will be \nseveral fast moving shortwave disturbances which will help aid \nin shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiods. The southward progress of the cold front is uncertain\nhowever, and could be modulated by various convective complexes\nand a potential wave of low pressure tracking along it Thursday\nor Friday. Both physics-based and AI guidance show lower severe\nweather chances Wednesday through Friday with declining \ninstability. Showers and thunderstorms could remain possible \nthough with the front nearby. Thursday may be relatively the \nhottest day as heights briefly rise.\n\nFor Saturday in particular, there may be a better chance of \ndrying out as high pressure builds to the north. This will also \nbring a corresponding cool down in temperatures (potentially a \nbit below normal) along with lower humidity. The front won't be \ntoo far to the south however, and will likely attempt to lift \nnorth later this weekend or early next week as a low pressure \nsystem moves into the Great Lakes. Thus, some low end PoPs \nremain in the forecast each day through the end of the forecast \nperiod.",
